In the setting of §2.11, let Y = X̄ ∖ (D′₊)°, the closure of X̄ ∖ D′₊ in X̄, with the scheme structure of the scheme-theoretic closure of X̄ ∖ D′₊ (as in Lemma 2.10.2). Put RΓ_c(X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋), F″) := RΓ_{∅,D₋}(Y, F″) = RΓ_{D₋,∅}(Y, F″), the cohomology of F″ on Y with compact support towards D₋; note X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋) ⊆ X″. There is a commutative diagram in which:
- the change-of-support maps RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) and RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″) both factor through RΓ_c(X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋), F″). As in the proof of Lemma 2.10.2, the maps into the middle term are restriction to Y (Lemma 2.2.7; D″₊ ∩ Y = ∅ since D″₊ ⊆ (D′₊)°). The maps out of it are the change of support of Lemma 2.2.6 on Y, from (∅, D₋ ∩ Y) to (D′₊ ∩ Y, D₋ ∩ Y), resp. from (D₋ ∩ Y, ∅) to (D₋ ∩ Y, D′₊ ∩ Y), followed by the identifications with RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″), resp. RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″). These identifications use the scheme structure chosen on Y;
- there are maps T : RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) and T : RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″), constructed as in Proposition 2.10.4(2) using p̄₂^⋆D′₊ ⊆ p̄₁^⋆D″₊ and p̄₁^⋆D₋ ⊆ p̄₂^⋆D₋;
- going around the diagram induces the endomorphism T of each term.
Consequently RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″)^{ord} = RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″)^{ord}.

items.note:
p. 59. It needs:
- coherent sheaves and Banach sheaves ([BP21] Definition 2.5.2) on rigid spaces, with cohomology supported in a closed subset;
- restriction and change-of-support maps;
- representation by (projective systems of) complexes of Banach modules ([BP21] Lemma 2.5.19);
- compact cohomological correspondences ([BP21] Definition 2.5.23).
AdicSpacesPartII R3 plans coherent sheaves on adic spaces, but local cohomology on them is planned nowhere. The extraction of Pilloni (2020) routes formal Banach sheaves to AdicSpacesPartII by a source route. The target p_1^!ℱ of the correspondence needs coherent upper shriek for finite flat maps, which the same extraction found unplanned.

items.statement:
Let S^tor be the toroidal compactification of level K^pK_{p,n} of §4.1, after the blow-up of Proposition 4.1.13, viewed as an adic space over Spa(Q_p, Z_p) with formal model 𝔖^tor. Here K_{p,n} ⊆ G(Z_p) is the Iwahori subgroup of elements reducing mod p^n into B(Z/p^nZ). For 1 ≤ i ≤ g let H_{g+i} ⊆ A[p^n] be the i-th group of the Iwahori flag, locally (Z/p^nZ)^i in the interior. Let deg H_{g+i}(x) ∈ [0, ni] be its Fargues degree at a rank-one point x. Its degree divisor D_{H_{g+i}} = Z_i = D_{g+1} + ⋯ + D_{g+i} is cut out locally on 𝔖^tor by a function f_{g+i}. Let w ∈ ^MW with sequence (w_1, …, w_g), where w_0 = 0 and w_i − w_{i−1} is 1 if g+i ∈ w^{−1}{g+1, …, 2g} and 0 otherwise (§3.1). Let ε ∈ Q. Define:
- 𝒰_{≥w−ε} = {x : |f_{g+i}|_x ≤ |p^{n(w_i−ε)}|_x for all i}, a quasi-compact open whose rank-one points satisfy deg H_{g+i}(x) ≥ n(w_i − ε) for all i;
- 𝒰_{≤w+ε}, the quasi-compact open whose rank-one points satisfy deg H_{g+i}(x) ≤ n(w_i + ε) for all i;
- 𝒵_{<w+ε} = closure of ∪_{ε′<ε} 𝒰_{≤w+ε′};
- 𝒵_{>w−ε} = closure of ∪_{ε′<ε} 𝒰_{≥w−ε′} (printed with ε′ > ε).
The last two are closed subsets with quasi-compact complements. Let t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p), i.e. t = diag(t_1, …, t_{2g}) with v(t_{g+1}) > ⋯ > v(t_{2g}) > v(t_1) > ⋯ > v(t_g). T denotes the Hecke correspondence C_t^tor with projections p_1, p_2, and T^t its transpose.

FL = P∖G → Spec Z_p is the flag variety, viewed as a scheme. For w ∈ ^MW:
- C_w = P∖PwB is the Bruhat cell;
- X_w = closure of C_w = ∪_{w′≤w} C_{w′};
- X^w = closure of P∖PwB^{op} (B^{op} the opposite Borel) is the opposite Schubert variety;
- Y_w = ∪_{w′≥w} C_{w′}, and there is an inclusion X^w ⊆ Y_w.
The Hodge–Tate period map gives a continuous map π_{HT,K_{p,n}} : |S^tor| → |FL|/K_{p,n} (§4.1.5). So every K_{p,n}-stable subset of FL pulls back to S^tor; examples are the tubes ]X_{w,F_p}[ and ]Y_{w,F_p}[ of the special fibres and their closures.

