items.statement:
[BP21] Lemma 3.5.10(5). Let K_p = K_{p,m′,b} with m′ ≥ b ≥ 0 and m′ > 0; the Iwahori subgroups K_{p,n} of this paper are the case m′ = n, b = 0. For t ∈ T^{++} and all m, n ∈ Z_{≥0},
]X_{w,k}[·(K_ptK_p)^m ∩ \overline{]Y_{w,k}[}·(K_pt^{−1}K_p)^n ⊆ ]C_{w,k}[_{m·min(t), \bar 0}K_p ∩ ]C_{w,k}[_{0, \overline{n·min(t)}}K_p.
Here \overline{]Y_{w,k}[} is the closure of the tube and min(t) = min_{α∈Φ^+} v(α(t)) > 0. The sets ]C_{w,k}[_{a,\bar b} and ]C_{w,k}[_{\bar a,b} are the partial closures of the neighbourhoods ]C_{w,k}[_{a,b} of the tube of the Bruhat cell ([BP21] §3.3.6). For t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p) one has min(t) ≥ 1, and these sets shrink as the indices grow. The paper therefore uses the lemma in the form T^m(π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}]X_{w,F_p}[) ∩ (T^t)^n(π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}\overline{]Y_{w,F_p}[}) ⊆ π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}(]C_{w,F_p}[_{m,\bar 0}K_{p,n} ∩ ]C_{w,F_p}[_{0,\bar n}K_{p,n}).

items.note:
§6.2.11, p. 63. The extension of ν_A to 𝔗_n for n large, i.e. the n-analyticity of a weight on a quasi-compact open of W, is planned: PadicMeasuresIwasawaAlgebras L0a ('with the prescribed analyticity condition for locally analytic weights') and OverconvergentAutomorphicForms O0 ('Prove a radius of analyticity locally uniformly in U'). Planned nowhere:
- the affinoid groups 𝒯_n and ℐ𝒲_n (the congruence subgroup of the Levi M reducing into M_{K_{p,P}});
- their formal models 𝔗_n and 𝔍𝔚_n, and the Borel subgroup 𝔅𝔍𝔚_n;
- the weight κ_A = −w_{0,M}w(ν_A + ρ) − ρ.

Remark 1.4.4 (p. 5) asserts this without proof: 'It holds if w = Id or w = w_0^M is the longest element of ^MW.' The item for Conjecture 5.6.1 mentions this claim only beside the conjecture, which must stay a Prop-valued statement. This item splits the proved cases off as a theorem.

The paper's own ingredients give the proof. By Remark 5.6.2 and the residual-fibre criterion (planned), it suffices to bound RΓ_w(K^pK_{w,p,P}, κ) ⊗ Z/pZ and its cuspidal version at Iwahori level, for every residual weight κ.
(1) For w = Id, the cuspidal complex lies in degree 0. Here D_{w,−} = ∅ (p. 58), so the complex is a direct summand of RΓ((Ig_{K^pK_{w,p,P}})_{Z/pZ}, ω^κ(−cusp)). The image of this Igusa variety in the minimal compactification is affine, and the higher direct images to the minimal compactification vanish ([Lan17], Thm. 8.6). This is the w = Id step on p. 58, and it does not use g = 2.
(2) For every w, both complexes have amplitude in [0, d], where d = g(g+1)/2. By Proposition 2.5.4 the residual complexes are direct summands of coherent cohomology on a closed subscheme of S^tor_{F_p}, which has dimension at most d. This gives the non-cuspidal bound for Id and the cuspidal bound for w_0^M.
(3) Take w = w_0^M = w_{0,M}w_0. Then w_{0,M}ww_0 = Id, so Theorem 5.5.2 gives M^•_{w_0^M} ≅ RHom_Λ(M^•_{Id,cusp}, Λ)[−d]. By (1) this has amplitude [d, d].

The residual-fibre criterion is planned. Every other input is missing:
- affineness of the ordinary locus in the minimal compactification;
- the relative vanishing;
- Proposition 2.5.4;
- Theorem 5.5.2.

routes.reason:
This paper builds higher Hida theory for the coherent cohomology of Siegel varieties of every genus. It develops ordinary cohomology with partial support for dynamic correspondences in the abstract (§2), integral Hecke operators at deep Iwahori level and a Cousin spectral sequence (§4), and admissible families over the whole weight space with control, duality and vanishing (§5). It then compares them with higher Coleman theory, with slope bounds and an application to Γ(N) (§6). PadicFamilies stops at ordinary and Coleman families in one degree for modular curves and totally real fields, so under PROTOCOL 15 this mathematics extends it as a Part II. PAPER-PILLONI-20 has already proposed that Part II, HigherHidaAndColemanTheory, for the Siegel threefold, and its brief names this paper as the source that extends the families to the whole weight space. This paper therefore joins that proposal instead of opening a second one.

The §2 formalism stays here: no atlas or draft layer plans compactified dynamic correspondences or ordinary parts of cohomology with partial support, and only this theory uses them. No atlas stage plans the Banach sheaves of [BP21] or the prime-to-p Hecke action on the Igusa complexes, which the paper uses without constructing it, so this roadmap plans both.

General inputs are imported from their owners:
- solid six functors and supports (AnalyticStacks AS.3);
- cohomological correspondences and ω^κ (IntegralCoherentHeckeComplexes);
- integral models: ShimuraVarietiesHondaTatePartII for the interior, ShimuraCompactificationsPartIIHodgeTypeIntegralBoundary for the toroidal models;
- ordinary Igusa formal schemes (IgusaVarietiesAndTorsionConcentrationPartIISiegelOrdinary);
- the Hasse invariant (HodgeTateAndCanonicalSubgroups T0);
- the equivariant coefficient sheaves (OverconvergentAutomorphicForms O1, O8);
- the Kostant set (ShimuraData D3, D5; AutomorphicFormsOnReductiveGroups AF.4);
- admissible duality (CompletedCohomologyPartII CC.3).

All of these roadmaps are upstream of this one, so no cycle arises.

These items are general coherent cohomology of automorphic bundles and of cohomological correspondences, not steps of the higher Hida argument. They are:
- solid cohomological correspondences, with their restriction to strata and completions (Proposition 2.9.9);
- coefficient change, pullback along Tor-independent squares, twists and the transpose (§§2.6–2.8);
- the torsor M_dR and the integral bundles ω^κ (§4.3.1);
- Lemma 2.6.11;
- Lan's relative vanishing;
- the Hecke action of G(A_f) on the complexes of §1.1;
- the automorphic description of coherent cohomology (Su, Harris).

PAPER-CALEGARI-GERAGHTY-18 proposed IntegralCoherentHeckeComplexes as the Part II of Automorphic bundles and classical automorphic forms (AutomorphicBundles) for these objects, and PAPER-CALEGARI-GERAGHTY-20 and PAPER-PILLONI-20 joined it. In that proposal:
- layer 1 owns Lan–Suh's integral bundles;
- layer 2 owns Nakajima's lemma;
- layer 3 owns cohomological correspondences, and already asks for restriction to strata, base change to formal completions and reduction modulo p^n;
- layer 5 owns Lan's theorem;
- layers 6–7 own Harris's comparison and discrete-series characters.
A separate roadmap would give these objects two owners, against PROTOCOL 15, so the route joins under the same id, parent and title.

Each notion is planned once, in the most general form its uses need.
- Cohomological correspondences take the solid form, for F ∈ D(O_{X,■}), which adds an import of Six-functor formalisms and analytic stacks (AnalyticStacks) AS.3.
- M_dR is the Levi torsor behind Lan–Suh's bundles, so one torsor and one functor serve both.
- Pullback along Tor-independent squares and twists by locally free sheaves are planned here because Hida and Coleman families, period modules, and family L-functions, Part II: higher Hida and higher Coleman theory for coherent cohomology (HigherHidaAndColemanTheory) imports them. It needs them for its iterated correspondences (§2.8) and its integral Hecke correspondences at p (§4.3).
- The characteristic-zero Hecke operators at p, which that roadmap uses to define ordinary parts, are planned here as well.

The set-theoretic contracted condition is the hypothesis of Proposition 2.9.9, and the expanded condition is its reverse. Both are owned here because this roadmap cannot import HigherHidaAndColemanTheory.

Dual Weyl modules come from LanglandsParameterStacks LP3, whose prerequisites include nothing downstream of this roadmap. They do not come from PotentialAutomorphyInfrastructure PA.1, which lies downstream of PadicFamilies, IgusaVarietiesAndTorsionConcentration and AutomorphicBundles itself.

The toroidal models at Iwahori level and their semi-abelian chain belong to ShimuraCompactificationsPartIIHodgeTypeIntegralBoundary, and the open models to ShimuraVarietiesHondaTatePartII; both are upstream imports.

Whatever depends on dynamic compactifications, ordinary parts, the paper's blow-ups or Igusa sheaves stays in HigherHidaAndColemanTheory, which imports this roadmap.

sourceIssues.reason:
Fargues' construction ([Far10], sect. 2) attaches an effective Cartier divisor to a generically étale finite locally free group scheme. The paper states only that Gr_i is finite flat over the interior (p. 41). Near the boundary, Gr_i is a subquotient of kernels of isogenies of semi-abelian schemes. Such a kernel is only quasi-finite flat, and in general Gr_i admits no finite flat extension at all. So 4.1.8 applies a construction outside its hypotheses and gives no argument at the boundary. Checked by direct computation for g = 1:

(1) n = 1, at the cusp where A = Tate(q) and H_2 = μ_p. Here A_2 = Tate(q^p), and the second step Tate(q^p) → Tate(q) is the quotient by ⟨q⟩. Its kernel Gr_1 has order p for q ≠ 0. On the boundary fibre q = 0 the map is the identity of G_m, so the kernel is trivial there. The order drops from p to 1, so Gr_1 is quasi-finite flat but not finite. In this case a different finite flat extension (the constant group Z/p) happens to exist.

(2) n = 2, at a cusp where H_2 ∩ μ_{p^2} = μ_p. H_2 = Gr_2 is cyclic of order p^2, generated by x = u·q^{1/p} with u a primitive p^2-th root of unity. It is defined over Q_p(ζ_p)((q)). The cusp has width 1: A/H_2 ≅ Tate(u^p q), and near the cusp S^tor is Z_p[ζ_p][[q]], with q a local equation of the boundary. The automorphism q^{1/p} ↦ ζ_p q^{1/p} fixing u lies in the inertia at q = 0 and sends x to ζ_p x = x^{1+pk} ≠ x. So Gr_2 is ramified along the boundary. It therefore has no finite flat extension even at the characteristic-0 boundary points, since there such an extension would be finite étale and hence unramified.

The divisors are then used on the boundary: in Lemma 4.1.9 and Proposition 4.1.13, in the normality argument of Corollary 4.2.10, and in Lemma 4.3.6. The proof of Lemma 4.3.6 has to show separately that the Gr_i extend to finite flat group schemes off Σ D_i ∩ D_{2g+1−i}. This confirms that no such extension was available when D_i was defined. The missing step is routine: use the determinant of f_i^* on invariant differentials of the semi-abelian schemes, which is essentially the preprint's co-Lie-complex definition applied to the quasi-finite kernel, together with a codimension-one argument. After this repair no stated result changes. The paper says itself (p. 41) that the Gr_i are finite flat only over the interior. §4.1.8 then defines D_i on the toroidal compactification by Fargues' construction for finite flat group schemes, and cites Fargues' Cartier-duality lemma for D_i + D_{2g+1−i} = V(p^n). Neither applies at the boundary. There the groups H_i/H_{i−1} are kernels of isogenies of semi-abelian schemes: they are quasi-finite and flat, not finite, and Gr_{2g+1−i} = Gr_i^D no longer makes sense.

Example, g = n = 1 (the modular curve X_0(p)). At the cusp 0, with parameter q^{1/p}, the subgroup ⟨q^{1/p}⟩ of the Tate curve G_m/q^Z has trivial fibre at the cusp, so it is not finite. The proof of Lemma 4.3.6 (p. 47) has to build finite flat extensions of the Gr_i by hand, which shows the authors know this.

Defence. The definition itself extends routinely. The paper constructs the chain of isogenies f_i of semi-abelian schemes over S^tor just before §4.1.8. The co-Lie complex of the kernel of an isogeny of smooth commutative group schemes is [ω_{A_i} → ω_{A_{i−1}}] with or without finiteness, so D_i := V(det Lie f_i) is defined on all of S^tor and has the stated étale-locus property. The 2024 preprint phrased §4.1.8 in exactly this co-Lie form. In the example, near the cusp 0:
- the isogeny E_q → E_{q^{1/p}} is the identity on G_m at the cusp, so D_2 is empty there;
- the isogeny E_{q^{1/p}} → E_q is x ↦ x^p, with kernel μ_p, so D_1 = V(p);
- hence D_1 + D_2 = V(p). A direct computation gives the same at the cusp ∞.

So the stated properties are true. What is missing is the boundary step for D_i + D_{2g+1−i} = V(p^n), where Fargues' lemma does not apply. The natural argument is Hartogs on the normal scheme S^tor. It needs the codimension-1 points of the special fibre to lie in the interior, which the paper never states. The paper assumes the same thing implicitly in the proof of Corollary 4.2.10 (p. 45): it reduces to generic points of the special fibre and treats the group schemes there as finite flat. So this is a genuine but fillable gap. It is not a false statement.

Uses. Every later result relies on the D_i near the boundary:
- Lemma 4.1.9 (its proof also uses Gr_{2g+1−i} = Gr_i^D);
- the blow-up of Proposition 4.1.13, whose proof uses D_g + D_{g+1} = V(p^n), for example to get Z_{1,<1} = D_g;
- Corollary 4.1.14 and the filtration in 4.1.16–4.1.18;
- Lemma 4.2.12, which begins "Granting that D_i + D_{2g+1−i} = (p^n)";
- Lemma 4.2.13 and Propositions 4.2.14 and 4.2.15;
- Lemma 4.3.6, Propositions 4.3.11 and 4.3.13, and Lemma 4.3.16;
- the divisors D_{w,±} in §§4.4–4.5, and so the control theorem 4.5.2.

All of these survive once the extension is justified, so the gap is in the proof, not in any stated result.

The Cousin filtration F_i = ∪_{ℓ(w)≤i} Z_{>w−1} and Lemma 4.5.7 (p. 52) run over all of ^MW. So the E_1 term RΓ_c(F̂_p \ F̂_{p−1}, ω̃^κ) = ⊕_{w,ℓ(w)=p}(…) is a sum over every w with ℓ(w) = p. Theorem 4.5.2 (p. 51), however, sums only over w ∈ C(κ). The only justification given for identifying the summands is the parenthetical before Proposition 4.5.8, which cites Corollary 2.3.11. That corollary (p. 17) only rewrites the colim_r lim_m of twisted cohomologies as cohomology with partial support along D_{−,w}, D_{+,w}. It says nothing about ordinary parts. RΓ_w(K^pK_{w,p,P}, κ) is defined differently (p. 51): through the closure compactification of Proposition 4.4.4, with the sheaf ω^κ and the operator t_w of Proposition 4.3.9. The right-hand side of Proposition 4.5.8 uses instead ω̃^κ = ω^κ(−k'ΣZ_i) on all of S^tor. Its operator is the single t̃ extending the t of Proposition 4.3.11, which is normalized by ⟨−w^{-1}w_{0,M}(κ+ρ)−ρ, t⟩ for w ∈ C(κ). The proof supplies neither the comparison of the two compactifications nor the comparison of (ω̃^κ, t̃) with (ω^κ, t_w).

The first comparison is routine. It needs no strictness over all of S^tor and no change of support conditions of the kind treated in §2.11. Definition 2.1.9 (p. 7) does not require X to be dense in X̄, and Lemma 4.2.11 (p. 45) shows that D_{+,w} is expanded and D_{−,w} is contracted. So the toroidal pair is a dynamic compactification of (ℭ^tor_{t,w}, 𝔖^tor_w) mod p^s, and the Proposition 4.4.4 compactification maps strictly to it. Proposition 2.2.11 (p. 15) then gives the equivariant identification, exactly as in the proof of Theorem 2.11.4(2) (p. 32).

The second comparison changes the statement. On 𝔖^tor_w both Z_{i,>w_i} and Z_{i,<w_i} are empty, so Z_i = V(p^{n w_i}) there (Proposition 4.1.13(1), p. 42). The twist by −k'ΣZ_i is therefore the same scalar on both sides. But by Remark 4.3.12 (p. 48), t restricted to ℭ^tor_{t,w} equals t_w only for w ∈ C(κ). For w ∉ C(κ) and t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p) it equals p^{n_w} t_w with n_w > 0. For such w the operator is nilpotent modulo each p^s, so the ordinary part of the w-summand is 0.

On the other hand, RΓ_w(K^pK_{w,p,P}, κ) is defined for every w ∈ ^MW. §4.4 uses the t_w of Proposition 4.3.9, which exists for all w, and T(Z_p) acts through ν = −w^{-1}w_{0,M}(κ+ρ)−ρ (Proposition 4.4.5, Remark 4.4.6). In general this complex is nonzero. For example, take g = 1 and w = Id. Then RΓ_Id computes Hida's ordinary p-adic modular forms of weight κ. By Hida's theory, an ordinary family of a given tame level specializes to a nonzero ordinary p-adic modular form in every weight of its component of weight space, and that component contains weights with C(κ) = {s}. In such a weight the Id-summand of the E_1 term has ordinary part 0, while RΓ_Id ≠ 0. So if the parenthetical is read as applying to every summand of the sum displayed just above it, it is false for w ∉ C(κ). What holds for those summands is that the ordinary part vanishes.

The needed vanishing is stated nowhere. C(κ) does not occur anywhere in §4.5.6 (pp. 52–53). Remark 4.3.12 is never cited in the paper, although its last sentence, "If t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p), n_{w'} > 0 for w' ∉ C(κ)", is exactly what is needed here.

Why this is a gap and not an error. The parenthetical is correct for the w that Theorem 4.5.2 keeps, namely w ∈ C(κ). For the other w, the statement the proof needs, the vanishing, is true. What is missing is a proof of each, and every ingredient is in the paper:
- Remark 4.3.12;
- Proposition 4.5.8, which holds for every w because its proof never uses w ∈ C(κ);
- Definition 2.4.2, with the argument of case (1) in the proof of Lemma 2.4.3;
- Lemma 2.4.4(1);
- the comparison above.
So the missing step is short, and Theorem 4.5.2 is correct as stated.

Corollaries 4.5.4 and 4.5.5 (C(κ) = {w}) need exactly this vanishing, for all lengths, so that the spectral sequence reduces to a single term. Corollary 4.5.5 is the classicality in regular weight listed as item (4) of Theorem 1.4.3, and Remark 1.4.5 says the spectral sequence degenerates because #C(κ) = 1. With the step supplied, these results stand. The same text is in the January 2024 preprint (pp. 50–51), so the omission was already there and the revision did not fix it.

